From 8efc220d4f5c654af043ff43689fa6917365909d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AnonGuard Review <anonguard-review@local>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 15:01:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Critical #1 (1-of-N directory trust) and Critical #2
 (relay cell loss under select!)

- consensus.rs: add ConsensusDocument::merge_signatures_from(), which lets a client
  combine independently-fetched, per-authority self-signed consensus documents into
  one multi-signed document (only when their content digests match). Each Directory
  Authority still only signs its own locally-built document; no authority-to-authority
  gossip round exists yet, so this is a client-side aggregation fix, not a full
  Tor-style voting protocol. Includes a regression test that reproduces the realistic
  per-authority-signs-its-own-copy scenario and shows quorum_threshold=2 was previously
  unreachable and is now reachable, plus a negative case for a disagreeing authority.

- main.rs: rewrite the consensus-fetch loop to query every configured authority each
  round (instead of breaking on the first responder), group fetched documents by
  content digest, merge signatures within each group, and run verify_quorum against
  the merged candidates. quorum_threshold now means what it says.

- gateway/server.rs: fix cancel-safety bug in the relay packet loop. Racing
  AsyncReadExt::read_exact directly inside tokio::select! is not cancel-safe: if the
  other branch resolves first, bytes already pulled off the socket by the losing
  read_exact future are silently dropped, corrupting the cell stream under concurrent
  load. Fixed by giving each direction (client, downstream) a persistent reader task
  that owns its read half for the connection's lifetime and forwards complete frames
  through an mpsc channel; mpsc::Receiver::recv() is cancel-safe, so select!-ing over
  channel recv() calls instead of raw socket reads is safe.

Known limitation / does not fully close Bug #1: this is client-side signature
aggregation, not authority-side voting. It makes quorum_threshold enforceable and
closes the trivial 1-of-N degradation, but does not yet give clients Byzantine
fault tolerance against a set of authorities that collude to both compute and sign
a false relay view. A proper fix needs an authority-to-authority round (leader-collect
or full gossip) so authorities cross-check each other's views before signing, not
just after the fact on the client.

NOT compiler-verified: rust-version=1.81 required, only rustc 1.75 was available via
apt in the sandbox this was written in, with no network path to rustup. Brace/paren
balance and control flow were checked manually; the cancel-safety refactor's borrow
pattern was checked by analogy to the pre-existing, structurally-identical code.
Run cargo check/test/clippy before merging.
